### Extension Formatting for the Opus Codec
Tim Terriberry presented [Opus Extension](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-mlcodec-opus-extension-00) regarding issues found in `draft-ietf-mlcodec-opus-extension`:
* **Issue**: The libopus repacketizer splits and recombines Opus frames. Repeated zero-byte extensions across up to 48 frames expand 1 byte of input into 48 struct descriptors (a 768x expansion factor). In large packets (e.g., Opus HD packets up to 20 KB), a flood of repeated zero-byte extensions could expand to ~15 MB of descriptors on the stack, causing stack overflows or crashes on memory-constrained/embedded systems.
* **Options Considered**:
  1. Imposing arbitrary limits on total extensions.
  2. Iterating parsers over the wire format without expanding struct lists (high computational complexity, $O(N \times \text{frames}^2)$).
  3. Special-casing zero-byte extensions in the implementation to collapse spans (adds significant code complexity).
  4. Ignoring multiple instances of the same zero-byte extension for a single frame.
  5. Having the repacketizer discard all unknown extensions.
* **Discussion**:
  * Mark Harris noted in chat that frame separators act as zero-byte extensions where order matters. Jean-Marc Valin and Tim Terriberry clarified that frame separators are handled separately and cannot be repeated.
  * Participants agreed that zero-byte extensions act as flags where multiple repetitions for the same frame carry no semantic value.
  * **Resolution**: Senders MUST NOT send duplicate zero-byte extensions for the same frame, and receivers/decoders SHOULD ignore them if received. The draft will also add security considerations detailing memory amplification risks during depacketization.

### Deep Audio Redundancy (DRED)
Jean-Marc Valin provided an update on `draft-ietf-mlcodec-opus-dred`:
* Signal confirmed successful production deployment of DRED since July with positive performance metrics.
* DRED payload sizes remain small (~200 bytes per second at max bitrates), making MTU issues non-existent in practice.
* Normative weights and hashes are present in the specification. The document is ready for shepherd write-up.

### Scalable Quality Extension (Opus HD)
Jean-Marc Valin presented updates on `draft-ietf-mlcodec-opus-scalable-quality-extension`:
* WGLC completed with positive reviews from Jan Skoglund and Jonathan Lennox.
* **Transport & MTU Discussion**:
  * High-bitrate modes (such as 900 kbps for 20 ms frames under Japanese Audio Society Hi-Res certification) produce packet sizes exceeding standard 1500-byte MTUs.
  * Jonathan Lennox noted that IP fragmentation across RTP often fails over NATs due to missing port numbers in subsequent fragments.
  * **Resolution**: The draft will be updated to include a warning regarding transport MTU constraints (such as RTP/NATs) and non-normative guidance suggesting shorter frame durations (e.g., 10 ms, 5 ms, 2.5 ms) to fit within standard MTUs.

### Speech Coding Enhancement
Jan Skoglund presented [Speech enhancement](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-mlcodec-speech-enhancement-00) covering updates in `draft-ietf-mlcodec-opus-speech-coding-enhancement` (-05 to -07):
* **SDP & Format Updates**: Shortened SDP parameters (`SNH` for speech enhancement, `XBW` for extending bandwidth); separated SDP and IANA considerations; pinned reference Opus archives and added a Python test verification script.
* **Threshold & Qualification Adjustments**:
  * Relaxed per-clip and group-mean objective test thresholds to prevent false failures for minor, non-audible degradations (validated with a small LACE model test during the Hackathon).
  * Introduced a MUSHRA subjective test override option: If isolated test clips fail objective criteria, a MUSHRA subjective test can override the failure provided the enhanced version rates significantly better than the unenhanced version (within a -20 point margin for single clips and -10 point margin for group means).
* **Next Steps**: Locate a permanent archival home for normative test vectors, update hashes/references, and proceed to WGLC.

